[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 15:58] No pricing constraint — price is on the table. Beyond pricing, what else could Brew and Bean do to rebuild margin? Give me a structured set of levers.

[candidate · 17:28] Three buckets beyond price.

One, input cost. Renegotiate supplier contracts and consolidate volume — 200 stores is real buying power. Lock in forward contracts or hedge green coffee rather than buying spot, which is likely what exposed them. Dual-source dairy and commoditize packaging away from branded cups.

Two, cost avoidance inside the same inputs. Reduce waste and shrink — brewed coffee dumped at end of day, spoiled milk. Right-size portions and standardize recipes. Audit free refills and comp policy.

Three, mix and revenue quality. Push high-margin items — food attach, drip coffee over milk-heavy espresso drinks, since dairy is in the inflating basket. Loyalty to drive attach rather than discount.

And reverse the overhead creep — 12 to 14 points is 2 points of margin sitting in corporate, unrelated to inflation.

One last thing before I recommend: does the CEO have a target margin and a timeframe? Whether we need 6 to 15 in a year or back to 24 eventually changes how aggressive I get on price.
