[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 17:32] No stated target — assume the CEO wants meaningful margin recovery over a reasonable horizon. Before we wrap, on what you asked about earlier: Menu prices have not changed in two years. So give me your recommendation to the CEO.

[candidate · 19:02] My recommendation: Brew & Bean should raise menu prices, phased, and pair it with supplier renegotiation. Margin didn't fall because the business got worse — it fell because roughly 38% input inflation was absorbed entirely by the P&L over two years while menu prices sat flat.

The math: COGS went 42 to 58 points of revenue. Beans up 40% explains 4.2 points; dairy, packaging and food up 37.5% explains 11.8. That's all 16. Meanwhile volume grew 15% with no price increase — evidence demand is healthy and there's likely headroom.

Sizing it: to restore COGS to 42% of revenue on today's cost base, we'd need roughly a 38% price increase, which is too steep at once. I'd phase 10–12% now, targeting about 6 points of margin, then reassess.

Risks: no elasticity data, so a step change could break traffic — test in 20 stores first. And competitors may have already priced up, which would mean we're under-market.

Next steps: price test, supplier renegotiation, hedge green coffee, and audit the overhead creep from 12 to 14.
